WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
A colony has survived an antibiotic.

2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.560
Does it carry the DNA you wanted?

3
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.480
Survival is encouraging, but it answers only one question.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.200
Follow one candidate insert through a chain that narrows what the colony might

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.200
contain.

6
00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:23.523
Start with a blue plasmid and a yellow DNA insert.

7
00:00:25.003 --> 00:00:28.683
Matching restriction cuts create compatible ends on both pieces.

8
00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:31.683
Their exposed bases can pair.

9
00:00:33.343 --> 00:00:34.863
DNA ligase seals the backbone.

10
00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:40.243
This creates a recombinant candidate, but other products can form too.

11
00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:46.623
Compatibility permits a join; it does not verify the joined pieces.

12
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:51.357
The plasmid carries an origin, a selectable marker, and a cloning site.

13
00:00:52.537 --> 00:00:54.877
The origin lets the host copy the circle.

14
00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:59.737
The marker can let plasmid-bearing cells survive an antibiotic.

15
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:02.917
The cloning site holds the insert.

16
00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:08.557
These parts make propagation possible, but none reads the insert's complete

17
00:01:08.557 --> 00:01:09.557
sequence.

18
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.660
Next, the plasmid enters bacteria by transformation.

19
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.900
Only some cells take up a circle.

20
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.480
Those cells divide, copying the plasmid as they grow.

21
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.440
Cells with no plasmid remain mixed among them.

22
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.720
The tube now contains several possibilities, so the experiment needs a way to narrow

23
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:32.720
the population.

24
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:38.033
Selection asks whether a cell probably carries a plasmid with the marker.

25
00:01:39.413 --> 00:01:40.973
Spread the cells on antibiotic.

26
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:44.073
Plasmid-free cells stop growing.

27
00:01:45.513 --> 00:01:47.133
Marker-bearing cells form colonies.

28
00:01:48.593 --> 00:01:51.133
Yet an empty vector can carry the same marker.

29
00:01:52.953 --> 00:01:57.913
Survival enriches plasmid-bearing cells; it does not identify the construct inside

30
00:01:57.913 --> 00:01:58.913
each colony.

31
00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:03.097
Several colonies remain, and their plasmids are hidden.

32
00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:09.077
Which next observation would separate an empty vector from the intended construct?

33
00:02:10.437 --> 00:02:11.497
Hold the plate in mind.

34
00:02:12.797 --> 00:02:18.197
You need evidence about the DNA inside a colony, not another test of antibiotic

35
00:02:18.197 --> 00:02:19.197
survival.

36
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.110
Screening inspects the candidate DNA.

37
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:34.010
A colony PCR or restriction digest can test whether fragments have expected sizes.

38
00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:39.390
A matching pattern moves a colony forward, but size is not sequence.

39
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.090
Screening changes the odds.

40
00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.330
It does not turn a candidate into proof.

41
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.140
Sequence both junctions and the insert to test the bases and orientation.

42
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.440
A correct sequence supports the intended construct.

43
00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.000
Expression is a separate question: the plasmid also needs a working promoter and

44
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.240
other expression parts.

45
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:08.180
Each result earns only the conclusion its measurement can support.

46
00:03:09.233 --> 00:03:10.713
The chain now makes sense.

47
00:03:12.053 --> 00:03:14.053
Compatible ends create possibilities.

48
00:03:15.573 --> 00:03:17.573
Transformation and growth create copies.

49
00:03:19.373 --> 00:03:21.593
Selection removes many cells without plasmids.

50
00:03:22.973 --> 00:03:24.433
Screening narrows the candidates.

51
00:03:25.873 --> 00:03:27.833
Sequencing tests the construct itself.

52
00:03:29.213 --> 00:03:34.253
Cloning succeeds by combining amplification with evidence, one inference at a time.
